//? Title: CYK: Derive Substrings, Recognize the Whole String, Recover One Parse
//? Subtitle: The original a^n b^n grammar, complete native charts, first witnesses and exact work counts
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
CYK recognizes a context-free language by combining derivations of shorter
substrings. For an epsilon-free grammar whose rules are A→BC or A→one byte,
it fills every positive-length span and accepts when the start derives the
whole input. We begin with the two-byte string ab, retain the original eight
examples for a^n b^n, then inspect the complete chart and one parse of aabb.
A deliberately ambiguous grammar separates recognition and first-witness
reconstruction from parse counting. All computations use datamunge's single
canonical core, with compressed arbitrary integer ids, owned actual witnesses,
checked dense geometry and explicit potential-work budgets. The usual cubic
bound fixes the grammar; chart space also depends on its nonterminal count.
Empty input, byte terminals, duplicate rules and unreachable symbols have
specified behavior rather than implicit grammar-conversion assumptions.
)

\toc

> Start with two terminal facts and one binary rule

Use the original ids S=0, A=1, B=2, C=3 and grammar
S→AB | AC, C→SB, A→a, B→b. The unary map in CNFGrammar means terminal-byte
productions, not nonterminal unit rules. For input ab, A derives the first
byte and B derives the second; the binary rule S→AB establishes S over the
whole two-byte span. The program reads this actual first witness and draws
the two child facts combining into their parent.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cyk_combine.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::CNFGrammar g;g.unary['a']={1};g.unary['b']={2};g.binary={{0,1,2},{0,1,3},{3,0,2}};
  const auto a=dm::algorithms::cyk_analyze(g,"ab");const auto tree=dm::algorithms::cyk_first_parse(a);
  const auto& e=dm::algorithms::cyk_cell(a,0,2).entries[0];
  if(!a.accepted || e.nonterminal!=0 || e.left_length!=1 || tree.size()!=3) return 1;
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"S","A","B","C"};
  std::print("| byte span | substring | deriving nonterminal ids |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(const auto& cell:a.cells) {
    std::vector<int> ids;for(const auto& entry:cell.entries) ids.push_back(entry.nonterminal);
    std::print("| [{},{}) | {} | {} |\n",cell.begin,cell.begin+cell.length,a.input.substr(cell.begin,cell.length),ids);
  }
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(78,1000);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3;box.height=1.3;box.font_size=20;box.fill={219,234,254};
  d.node("left",{0,0},std::format("{} derives a\n[0,1)",names[e.left]),box)
   .node("right",{5,0},std::format("{} derives b\n[1,2)",names[e.right]),box);
  box.fill={220,252,231};d.node("parent",{2.5,3.2},std::format("{} derives ab\n[0,2)",names[e.nonterminal]),box);
  d.edge("left","parent","left child").edge("right","parent","right child")
   .text({2.5,5},std::format("rule {}: {} -> {}{}; split after {} byte",e.binary_rule,names[e.nonterminal],names[e.left],names[e.right],e.left_length),22)
   .text({2.5,-1.8},std::format("accepted {}; first parse has {} nodes",a.accepted,tree.size()),20)
   .save_svg("build/cyk_combine.svg");
  std::print("\nAccepted {}; cells {}; split points {}; actual binary rule tests {}; new memberships {}.\n",a.accepted,a.cells.size(),a.work.split_points,a.work.binary_rule_tests,a.work.new_states);
}
