// result_end
\caption(A two-child rule turns two established substring facts into one larger fact. The split and original rule index come from the executing parser's first witness.)
\alttext(Two lower boxes say A derives a on span zero to one, and B derives b on span one to two. Arrows combine them into S derives ab on span zero to two.)

> Derive the recurrence and why it is correct

Let T(i,ℓ) be the set of nonterminals deriving the half-open byte span
[i,i+ℓ). Terminal rules provide the base row. Every longer binary-normal-form
parse has a root rule A→BC and a positive split k between its two child yields:

\[
T(i,1)=\{A:A\to s_i\},\qquad
T(i,\ell)=\bigcup_{k=1}^{\ell-1}
\{A:A\to BC,\ B\in T(i,k),\ C\in T(i+k,\ell-k)\}.
\]
\alttext(Length-one memberships come from terminal productions. A longer membership needs a binary rule and a split whose left and right nonterminals occur in the two shorter cells.)

Soundness follows by induction on ℓ: a terminal entry has its literal rule,
and combining two valid child derivations with A→BC gives a valid parent.
For completeness, any parse of a longer span has such a root and split;
its shorter child spans occur by induction, so the loop visits their split
and rule and inserts the parent. Children are strictly shorter, making the
dependency graph acyclic even when the grammar itself has recursive rules.
Acceptance is exactly start∈T(0,n) for n>0.

The implementation loops in increasing length, begin, split and original
binary-rule index. Dense memberships are compressed by sorted distinct ids,
so negative ids and INT_MIN/INT_MAX do not imply huge id-indexed arrays.
Each first insertion stores its split and original rule index. Later
alternatives leave that first witness intact. Public cell entries are
extracted in increasing original-id order; a grammar's rule-vector order can
change the chosen parse without changing the recognized language.

The represented grammar has no epsilon rule. Empty input therefore rejects,
including a default grammar with no productions. General CFG-to-normal-form
conversion, removal of nonterminal unit rules and any special treatment of
an epsilon-producing start are separate operations not supplied by this API.
A start symbol on a binary right-hand side is valid here because this grammar
is epsilon-free.

> Retain the original eight strings

The grammar generates exactly a^k b^k for k≥1. Expanding S→AB gives ab.
Expanding S→AC and then C→SB wraps one a around a smaller S and one b:
S⇒aSb. Induction gives only a^k b^k; repeated wrapping constructs every such
string. In particular, equal counts alone do not suffice: all a bytes must
precede all b bytes. This is a nonregular language in the formal-language
sense. If a finite automaton pumped a nonempty substring of the initial a
block of a^p b^p, pumping would change the number of a bytes while leaving
the b count fixed, contradicting recognition of exactly this language.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/cyk_accept.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::algorithms::CNFGrammar g;g.unary['a']={1};g.unary['b']={2};g.binary={{0,1,2},{0,1,3},{3,0,2}};
  const std::vector<std::string> strings{"ab","aabb","aaabbb","aab","abb","ba","aabbb","aaaabbbb"};
  const std::vector<bool> expected{true,true,true,false,false,false,false,true};std::vector<double> accepted;
  std::print("| original input | accepted |\n| :--- | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<strings.size();++i) {
    const bool member=dm::algorithms::cyk_recognize(g,strings[i]);if(member!=expected[i]) return 1;
    accepted.push_back(member?1:0);std::print("| {} | {} |\n",strings[i],member);
  }
  auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::barplot(accepted,strings,"accepted",{22,163,74});
  plot.title("Original grammar: four accepted, four rejected").x_label("input byte string").y_label("accepted = 1, rejected = 0").y_limits(0,1.25).size(1050,520)
      .save_svg("build/cyk_accept.svg");
  const bool empty=dm::algorithms::cyk_recognize(g,""),unknown=dm::algorithms::cyk_recognize(g,"ax"),wrong_order=dm::algorithms::cyk_recognize(g,"abab");
  if(empty || unknown || wrong_order) return 1;
  std::print("\nOriginal accepted strings: 4 of 8. Empty input {}; unknown byte ax {}; equal-count wrong-order abab {}.\n",empty,unknown,wrong_order);
}
