\image(figures/cyk_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The all-a grammar reaches the worst-case candidate loop at every split. Separate axes retain both the quadratic state count and the cubic rule-test count. The statement that every split succeeds concerns a chart combination, rather than the number of complete parse trees.)
\alttext(Two panels plot actual memberships and actual binary rule tests against lengths from one to 128. Memberships end at 8256 and rule tests at 349504.)

Grammar preparation collects raw ids, sorts them and compiles byte/rule
indices. Let G count raw grammar ids including repetitions, and U be the
number of matching terminal candidates actually visited over the input.
This implementation takes
O(G log(G+1)+Qm+K(1+r)+U) time and O(G+Qm) storage; U≤nG. Dense initialization
and complete chart extraction both account for Qm. The split loop accounts
for K even if r=0, and duplicate terminal candidates still contribute to U.
For a fixed grammar this gives the usual O(n³) bound. The O(n²) space shorthand
also fixes the grammar; in grammar-dependent terms it is O(n²m), not a
constant amount of data per span independent of the number of symbols.
First-tree recovery is separate, with at most O(nm) local entry searches and
O(n) nodes/tasks, and does not rerun the chart dynamic program.

The analysis defaults to at most 1,000,000 potential state slots and
100,000,000 potential binary-rule tests. Preparation comes first, then checked
geometry and complete budgets, then input/chart/choice allocation and parsing.
Caps count Qm and Kr, including unreachable ids and repeated binary rules;
they do not depend on how many states later succeed. A rejected bbbb can
therefore exceed a potential-work cap even when empty children would skip
every rule test. Zero disables the corresponding optional cap, with checked
size arithmetic and container limits still enforced. First-parse output has
its own complete 100,000-node cap. No result silently truncates. The original
inline recognizer remains uncapped, uses the same prepared membership kernel,
and does not capture chart entries or work counters.

A terminal is one char byte and positions count bytes, including embedded
NULs. UTF-8 text is a byte sequence here, not a sequence of decoded Unicode
characters or lexical tokens. Grammar conversion and tokenization need their
own explicitly chosen representation; the API does not infer them.

The permanent native tests check the original cases and every one of 8191
binary strings through length twelve against the proven a^k b^k language.
An independent bounded-language generator closes terminal sets under grammar
concatenation, rather than parsing fixed substrings: 100 seeded grammars and
127 strings each give 12700 comparisons, including recursive, unproductive,
ambiguous, duplicate-rule and sparse-id grammars. Recovered nodes are checked
against actual original productions and terminal bytes. All 256 byte values,
embedded NUL and a two-byte UTF-8 fixture, exact split enumeration, factor-
cancelled large geometry, complete budgets, owned results, checked borrows,
malformed visited witnesses and a 200-byte right-branching parse are covered.
The nine new cases pass AddressSanitizer and UBSan. These are formal grammar
controls, so no unrelated external dataset is required.

> References and reproducibility

- D. H. Younger (1967), “Recognition and parsing of context-free languages
  in time n³,” Information and Control 10(2), pp. 189–208:
  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0019-9958(67)80007-X .
- T. Kasami, “An Efficient Recognition and Syntax-Analysis Algorithm for
  Context-Free Languages,” University of Illinois Coordinated Science
  Laboratory report archived as March 1966:
  https://www.ideals.illinois.edu/items/100444 .

Each block is a complete editable C++23 program using dm/dm.hpp and the
working canonical library. Native SVGs are saved in local build/ and copied
into figures/. Results contain tables and image references rather than SVG
XML, data URIs or base64 payloads. The boolean API is recognition; actual
stored first choices additionally support one recovered parse, while
ambiguity counts and probabilistic inside/Viterbi variants remain distinct
algorithms rather than claims made by these outputs.
